Purpose: Digital assets integrate trading, payments, custody, exchanges, and data trusts. To map this structural shift, we compare how five jurisdictions statutorily articulate these functions, establishing a comparative baseline to pinpoint South Korea's governance trajectory.&nbsp; 
<div>
 Design/methodology/approach: Moving past qualitative reviews, we built a corpus from eight official frameworks across the US, EU, Japan, Singapore, and Korea (439 units; 72,076 tokens). Our text-as-data approach combines TF-IDF keyword profiling with a theoryguided four-dimensional strategy dictionary, supported by exploratory topic modeling as an auxiliary robustness check.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Findings: National regulatory focus diverges sharply. The US prioritizes Market Formation, whereas Singapore emphasizes Regulatory Authority. Conversely, Korea remains anchored in Trust/Protection. Tellingly, Korea's exceptionally low Ecosystem Coordination score indicates a statutory environment fixated on user defense over holistic market-building.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Research limitations/implications: Evaluating statutory wording captures policy intent, not implementation reality. Bridging the gap between these text-derived regulatory profiles and actual on-the-ground enforcement outcomes remains a crucial task for subsequent research.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Practical implications: While strict user protection provides an essential "trust floor" for post-catch-up economies, evolving into functional digital infrastructures requires layering on proactive mechanisms-namely, comprehensive licensing, custody standards, and technical coordination.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Social implications: Digital-asset rules do more than mitigate financial risks; they fundamentally structure public confidence and dictate how emerging technologies integrate into everyday societal infrastructure.
</div>

Background. The Riyadh Charter on Artificial Intelligence for the Islamic World articulates normative principles for AI governance grounded in Islamic ethical traditions, but acknowledges the absence of implementation templates, technical toolkits, and legislative guidance needed to translate these principles into national policy.&nbsp;
<div>
 Objective. This paper proposes the Islamic Human-in-the-Loop (I-HITL) framework as a policy architecture for operationalising the Charter's principles at the national and public-sector level.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Methods. The framework synthesises the Charter's principles with Islamic ethical concepts, including Karama, Adl, Amanah, Maslahah, La Darar, Sidq/Tabayyun, Khilafah, and Hisab, the five objectives of Maqasid al-Shariah, and comparative AI governance literature including the UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of AI, the OECD AI Principles, the EU AI Act, and the NIST AI Risk Management Framework.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Results. I-HITL develops a nine-layer governance model comprising normative foundations, Maqasid alShariah objectives, institutional mechanisms, risk classification, human oversight mandates, cultural and religious safeguards, operational governance requirements, accountability and redress mechanisms, and assurance metrics.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Conclusion. The framework provides an implementation pathway through which OIC member states may translate the Riyadh Charter's normative commitments into operational governance structures while maintaining interoperability with global AI governance frameworks. A companion paper on Decision Accountability Architecture (DAA) addresses the corresponding organizational-level implementation layer.&nbsp;
</div>

